Title:
Shellcode Execution via Thread Creation

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This write-up explains a basic Windows shellcode execution pattern using a newly created thread. It walks through allocating memory for a payload with `VirtualAlloc`, copying/decrypting shellcode into that buffer, changing page permissions with `VirtualProtect`, and executing it via `CreateThread` (or alternatively by casting the buffer to a function pointer). The post adds Windows internals context around `MEM_RESERVE`/`MEM_COMMIT`, including how reservations are tracked in the per-process VAD tree and how demand paging backs committed pages. It explicitly notes the technique is not stealthy and is commonly detected by EDR due to RW→RX transitions and thread start at non-module memory. The included full code sample demonstrates AES-CBC decryption of an embedded payload before execution and basic cleanup (`WaitForSingleObject`, `VirtualFree`). Useful for malware dev learners, red teamers building loaders, and defenders wanting to understand common telemetry patterns.

Technical Focus:
- WinAPI shellcode loader chain (`VirtualAlloc` → `VirtualProtect` → `CreateThread`)
- Memory allocation semantics (`MEM_RESERVE`, `MEM_COMMIT`, demand paging)
- Windows VAD tree / Virtual Address Descriptors and commit charge
- Page protection transitions (RW to RX / RWX) and execution permissions
- Thread-based execution vs direct function-pointer invocation
- Embedded payload decryption (AES-CBC) prior to in-memory execution

Use Cases:
- Implementing a minimal in-process shellcode loader for lab/testing
- Teaching Windows memory manager concepts relevant to injection/loaders
- Generating detection ideas for RW→RX transitions and suspicious thread starts
- Baseline comparison for more advanced techniques (e.g., indirect syscalls, APC, section mapping)

Keywords:
VirtualAlloc, VirtualProtect, CreateThread, WaitForSingleObject, VirtualFree, MEM_RESERVE, MEM_COMMIT, PAGE_READWRITE, PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE, PAGE_EXECUTE_READ, VAD, Virtual Address Descriptor, demand paging, PTE, Windows Memory Manager, shellcode loader, thread start routine, function pointer execution, AES-CBC, in-memory decryption, EDR detection, RWX, RW-to-RX transition